==================================================
RATING METHODOLOGY
==================================================

Create a transparent rating methodology.

The initial baseline should be relative to league average.

For example:

League average = 1000

Team above league average attack > 1000
Team below league average attack < 1000

Team above league average defence > 1000
Team below league average defence < 1000

IMPORTANT:

Clearly define what a HIGH defensive rating means.

Do not create ambiguity where:

"higher defensive rating" could mean either "better defence" or "more goals conceded."

Document the convention in code.

The projection engine must use the convention consistently.

==================================================
HOME / AWAY
==================================================

If sufficient data exists, maintain separate home and away strengths.

For example:

Team A:
Home Attack = 1120
Away Attack = 980

Team A:
Home Defence = 1080
Away Defence = 1010

Do not create separate ratings merely for the sake of it if there is insufficient data.

If the sample is too small, use a sensible regression toward the league average rather than producing extreme ratings.

==================================================
SMALL SAMPLE HANDLING
==================================================

This is important.

Do NOT allow teams with very little data to receive extreme ratings.

Use a simple shrinkage approach toward league average.

For example:

rating = weighted(team_observed_strength, league_average)

where the weight increases with sample size.

Document the exact method.

Do not build an ML model for this.

This is still the deterministic/statistical baseline.

